WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.770
In this lesson you'll learn what a file really is,

2
00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.730
and what the little ending on its name,

3
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.190
like .py, actually does.

4
00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.114
Every file is just a row of numbers called bytes,

5
00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:18.086
and the extension is part of its name:

6
00:00:18.086 --> 00:00:20.513
a label that tells your computer

7
00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:23.148
which program to open it with,

8
00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:25.018
not what is inside.

9
00:00:25.018 --> 00:00:26.933
Agents read and write your files now,

10
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:30.511
so this keeps them from surprising you.

11
00:00:30.961 --> 00:00:33.701
This is the first stop on the map:

12
00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:36.046
District One, The Machine,

13
00:00:36.046 --> 00:00:38.763
which asks what's on your computer.

14
00:00:38.763 --> 00:00:41.886
Aaron, who made this course, has an IT degree.

15
00:00:41.886 --> 00:00:44.278
He still wrote Python in Notepad,

16
00:00:44.278 --> 00:00:48.202
and saved it as .txt.

17
00:00:48.202 --> 00:00:49.839
He didn't know that renaming

18
00:00:49.839 --> 00:00:52.161
it to .py was the whole difference.

19
00:00:52.161 --> 00:00:54.204
Nothing inside had to change.

20
00:00:54.204 --> 00:00:55.669
Only the label.

21
00:00:56.131 --> 00:00:58.407
Nobody handed you this part.

22
00:00:58.407 --> 00:01:00.357
That's not a character flaw;

23
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:03.169
it's a missing semester.

24
00:01:03.618 --> 00:01:06.021
Picture a file as a string of beads.

25
00:01:06.021 --> 00:01:08.007
Each bead is one byte:

26
00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:11.223
a number from zero to two hundred fifty-five.

27
00:01:11.223 --> 00:01:14.055
A photo, a song, a spreadsheet,

28
00:01:14.055 --> 00:01:16.006
your Python script:

29
00:01:16.006 --> 00:01:17.898
beads, in order.

30
00:01:17.898 --> 00:01:20.685
Tied to the string is a paper tag.

31
00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:22.357
That's the file's name,

32
00:01:22.357 --> 00:01:26.142
and the part after the last dot is the extension.

33
00:01:26.142 --> 00:01:28.533
Your computer stores and moves beads.

34
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:31.135
It never knows what they mean.

35
00:01:31.586 --> 00:01:34.477
Numbers become words through an agreement:

36
00:01:34.477 --> 00:01:35.917
a code card that says

37
00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:38.389
which number means which letter.

38
00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:41.677
One hundred twelve means a small p.

39
00:01:42.116 --> 00:01:43.869
Lay the card over our beads,

40
00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:46.237
and they spell print, hi.

41
00:01:46.237 --> 00:01:49.047
A .py file is just text

42
00:01:49.047 --> 00:01:51.624
whose letters happen to be Python.

43
00:01:51.624 --> 00:01:54.016
Nothing in the beads says Python.

44
00:01:54.016 --> 00:01:55.839
Use the wrong card, and the same

45
00:01:55.839 --> 00:01:58.556
beads come out as garbled symbols.

46
00:01:58.556 --> 00:02:02.271
Today's card is almost always UTF eight,

47
00:02:02.271 --> 00:02:05.742
used on about ninety-nine percent of websites.

48
00:02:05.742 --> 00:02:08.273
Python three point fifteen, due this month,

49
00:02:08.273 --> 00:02:10.352
makes it the default everywhere.

50
00:02:10.352 --> 00:02:13.140
And what you see is a rendering:

51
00:02:13.742 --> 00:02:17.410
newer Notepad on Windows 11 can show bold text

52
00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.280
that is really stars in the file.

53
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.390
When you double-click, Windows

54
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.492
and Mac read the extension to choose

55
00:02:24.492 --> 00:02:26.651
which program opens the file.

56
00:02:26.651 --> 00:02:31.910
Rename hello.txt to hello.py,

57
00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.861
and not one bead moves.

58
00:02:33.861 --> 00:02:36.624
Programs themselves mostly ignore the tag:

59
00:02:36.624 --> 00:02:39.782
Python will happily run notes.txt.

60
00:02:39.782 --> 00:02:41.895
Many other formats carry a stamp

61
00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:43.706
in their first few beads.

62
00:02:43.706 --> 00:02:46.667
PNG pictures all start with the same few bytes,

63
00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.768
and so do PD Fs.

64
00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.612
A modern Word file is a zip inside.

65
00:02:51.612 --> 00:02:55.200
The stamp is a far better clue than the tag.

66
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.684
Plain text has no stamp.

67
00:02:57.684 --> 00:02:59.344
Here's where the picture breaks:

68
00:02:59.344 --> 00:03:01.643
the tag isn't tied to the string.

69
00:03:01.643 --> 00:03:03.698
The folder keeps the name separately,

70
00:03:03.698 --> 00:03:06.358
so renaming is instant.

71
00:03:06.809 --> 00:03:09.363
Windows and Mac still hide most extensions

72
00:03:09.363 --> 00:03:11.012
by default, so a file that looks

73
00:03:11.012 --> 00:03:18.117
like hello.py can really be hello.py.txt.

74
00:03:18.117 --> 00:03:23.562
Renaming photo.png to .jpg doesn't convert it.

75
00:03:23.562 --> 00:03:26.325
Odd symbols where an accented letter belongs

76
00:03:26.325 --> 00:03:28.891
mean the wrong code card was used.

77
00:03:28.891 --> 00:03:31.016
Double-click a .py file,

78
00:03:31.016 --> 00:03:34.243
and what opens depends on the machine.

79
00:03:34.243 --> 00:03:39.375
And a file named invoice.pdf.exe

80
00:03:39.375 --> 00:03:41.998
is a program in a costume.

81
00:03:41.998 --> 00:03:44.959
A tag can lie, which is why attackers

82
00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:47.422
like hidden extensions.

83
00:03:47.872 --> 00:03:49.416
Five years ago, you made

84
00:03:49.416 --> 00:03:51.750
and named every file yourself, and AI

85
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.535
was autocomplete inside the one you had open.

86
00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:59.087
Now coding agents like Claude Code create,

87
00:03:59.087 --> 00:04:02.906
read and edit the files in your project directly.

88
00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:06.633
They read text as text and pictures as pictures.

89
00:04:06.633 --> 00:04:11.591
AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md are plain text

90
00:04:11.591 --> 00:04:14.040
whose names say: read me first.

91
00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.014
Same trick as an extension.

92
00:04:16.014 --> 00:04:18.951
AGENTS.md is now looked

93
00:04:18.951 --> 00:04:21.099
after by the Linux Foundation.

94
00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:23.781
Google uses an AI model that identifies

95
00:04:23.781 --> 00:04:26.254
files from their bytes, not their names.

96
00:04:26.254 --> 00:04:27.589
The direction looks like this:

97
00:04:27.589 --> 00:04:30.248
the name stays the cheap first guess,

98
00:04:30.248 --> 00:04:32.756
and more tools check the beads too.

99
00:04:32.756 --> 00:04:35.472
And a text file can hold invisible characters

100
00:04:35.472 --> 00:04:38.191
that an agent reads and you don't.

101
00:04:38.641 --> 00:04:40.580
Safe to ignore for now:

102
00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.749
hex, bits, UTF sixteen,

103
00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:47.499
byte order marks and line endings. Keep this.

104
00:04:47.499 --> 00:04:49.903
One: a file is just bytes.

105
00:04:49.903 --> 00:04:52.445
Two: text is bytes plus an agreement,

106
00:04:52.445 --> 00:04:55.556
and the agreement is usually UTF eight.

107
00:04:55.556 --> 00:04:57.867
Three: the extension is a label

108
00:04:57.867 --> 00:05:00.653
that picks the program, not the contents.

109
00:05:00.653 --> 00:05:04.347
Next on the map: file extensions and associations.
